<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/social-psychology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Social psychology measures <strong>how presence of others — real, imagined or implied —
    changes behaviour</strong>. Its findings are consistently humbling: ordinary situations move
    ordinary people further than personality does, and our self-narratives rationalise behaviour
    more often than they cause it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="situational power, from obedience to dissonance" widget="equationStep" config={socialCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts with daily leverage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stereotype threat:</strong> reminding a group of a negative stereotype measurably depresses performance (Steele & Aronson, 1995) — context design, not just individual grit, changes outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Bystander effect:</strong> more witnesses → less helping (Darley & Latané, 1968, after Kitty Genovese's case — whose reporting was itself exaggerated, a lesson in media psychology). Diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism; naming it enables action.</li>
    <li><strong>Contact hypothesis:</strong> Allport (1954) — cooperation on equal terms reduces prejudice. Meta-analyses (Pettigrew & Tropp, 2006, 500+ studies) confirmed it. The theory behind integrated schools and teams.</li>
    <li><strong>The replication caveat:</strong> some classic social priming effects weakened on retest; the big situational findings (conformity, dissonance) held. Social psychology audited itself — the methods lesson's field study.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Milgram (1963); Asch (1951); Festinger (1957); Pettigrew & Tropp (2006); the sociology ladder connects via Durkheim.</p>
</TopicPage>
